<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Gegodog</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1676">1676</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trunajaya-rebellion">Trunajaya rebellion</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Gegodog was fought in October 1676 near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuban">Tuban</a> on the north coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trunajaya-rebellion">Trunajaya rebellion</a>. Madurese and Makassarese forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trunajaya">Trunajaya</a> defeated a large Mataram army led by the crown prince, the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amangkurat-ii">Amangkurat II</a>. The defeat opened the way for the rebels' capture of the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plered">Plered</a> in 1677 and the flight of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amangkurat-i">Amangkurat I</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Madurese nobleman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trunajaya">Trunajaya</a> had lived in exile in Mataram, where in 1670 his father-in-law Raden Kajoran introduced him to the crown prince, Pangeran Adipati Anom. Both bore a grudge against King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amangkurat-i">Amangkurat I</a>: the prince over the execution of his maternal grandfather <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pangeran-pekik">Pangeran Pekik</a>, Trunajaya over his exile and his father's execution. In 1670 or 1671 Trunajaya returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madura">Madura</a> and, using the prince's patronage and his family's name, took control of the island.</p><p>The rebellion began in 1674 with raids on cities under Mataram. In 1676 a rebel army of 9,000 East Javanese, Madurese and Makassarese, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trunajaya">Trunajaya</a> and his Makassarese ally Karaeng Galesong, invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-java">East Java</a> from its base in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madura">Madura</a> and soon took Surabaya, the principal city of East Java.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The king sent a much larger army, mostly conscripted peasants with West Javanese auxiliaries, under the crown prince; he was either unaware of the prince's part in the rebellion or meant to have him killed on campaign. With it went other princes, among them the king's uncle Pangeran Purbaya, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan-agung">Sultan Agung</a>'s last surviving brother, nearly 80 years old, and the crown prince's bitter enemy Pangeran Singasari. The army marched by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jepara">Jepara</a> east into rebel territory and met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trunajaya">Trunajaya</a> at Gegodog.</p><p>The crown prince had expected a sham fight, after which both forces would turn on the king, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trunajaya">Trunajaya</a> betrayed him, and the presence of the other princes may have kept him from faking the battle. After long wavering he ordered the attack on 13 October. Trunajaya fought in earnest, and the royal army fared badly. Purbaya rallied the troops for a last charge; according to Thomas Stamford Raffles he fought on foot after his horse was shot under him, until he was overpowered and killed. The army broke and fell back towards the capital.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Defections to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trunajaya">Trunajaya</a> quickened, and by January 1677 nearly all of Mataram's north coast, except <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jepara">Jepara</a>, held by the king's governor with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">VOC</a> forces from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavia">Batavia</a>, was in rebel hands; towns as far west as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cirebon">Cirebon</a> submitted. Mataram could now fight only on the defensive and in the end had to seek the Dutch East India Company's help. The crown prince, blamed and accused of collusion, was replaced in command by his brothers Pangeran Martasana and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakubuwana-i">Pangeran Puger</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Gegodog” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Later rulers could not command such armies: the forces of Amangkurat I were defeated by Trunajaya’s rebels at <b>Gegodog</b> in 1676, and from 1677 the dynasty depended on Company troops.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mataram-sultanate">Mataram Sultanate · Military</a></li></ul></section>
